 * 【28†Members†pytorch.org】\nL58:     * 【29†Governing Board†pytorch.org】\nL59:     * 【30†Technical Advisory Council†pytorch.org】\nL60:     * 【31†Cloud Credit Program†pytorch.org】\nL61:     * 【32†Staff†pytorch.org】\nL62:     * 【33†Contact†pytorch.org】\nL63: Back to top [Input] [Input]\nL64: \nL65: [Input: Search the docs ...] `Ctrl`+`K`\nL66: # torch.Tensor.scatter_add_【35†#】\nL67: \nL68: Tensor.scatter_add_(dim, index, src) → 【36†Tensor】【37†#】\nL69: \nL70: \nL71: Adds all values from the tensor `src` into `self` at the indices specified in the `index` tensor in a similar fashion as 【38†`scatter_()`】. For each value in `src`, it is added to an index in `self` which is specified by its index in `src` for `dimension != dim` and by the corresponding value in `index` for `dimension = dim`.\nL72: For a 3-D tensor, `self` is updated as:\nL73: \nL74:     self[index[i][j][k]][j][k] += src[i][j][k]  # if dim == 0\nL75:     self[i][index[i][j][k]][k] += src[i][j][k]  # if dim == 1\nL76:     self[i][j][index[i][j][k]] += src[i][j][k]  # if dim == 2\nL77: `self`, `index` and `src` should have same number of dimensions. It is also required that `index.size(d) <= src.size(d)` for all dimensions `d`, and that `index.size(d) <= self.size(d)` for all dimensions `d != dim`. Note that `index` and `src` do not broadcast. When `index` is empty, we always return the original tensor without further error checking.\nL78: \nL79: Note\nL80: \nL81: This operation may behave nondeterministically when given tensors on a CUDA device. See 【39†Reproducibility】 for more information.\nL82: \nL83: Note\nL84: The backward pass is implemented only for `src.shape == index.shape`.\nL85: \nL86: Parameters\nL87: \nL88: \nL89:   * dim (【40†int†docs.python.org】) – the axis along which to index\nL90: \nL91:   * index (LongTensor) – the indices of elements to scatter and add, can be either empty or of the same dimensionality as `src`. When empty, the operation returns `self` unchanged.\nL92: \nL93:   * src (【36†Tensor】) – the source elements to scatter and add\nL94: Example:\nL95: \nL96:     >>> src = torch.ones((2, 5))\nL97:     >>> index = torch.tensor([[0, 1, 2, 0, 0]])\nL98:     >>> torch.zeros(3, 5, dtype=src.dtype).scatter_add_(0, index, src)\nL99:     tensor([[1., 0., 0., 1., 1.],\nL100:             [0., 1., 0., 0., 0.],\nL101:             [0., 0., 1., 0., 0.]])\nL102:     >>> index = torch.tensor([[0, 1, 2, 0, 0], [0, 1, 2, 2, 2]])\nL103:     >>> torch.zeros(3, 5, dtype=src.dtype).scatter_add_(0, index, src)\nL104:     tensor([[2., 0., 0., 1., 1.],\nL105:             [0., 2., 0., 0., 0.],\nL106:             [0., 0., 2., 1., 1.]])\nL107: Rate this Page\nL108: \nL109: ★ ★ ★ ★ ★\nL110: \nL111: [Button: Send Feedback ]\nL112: \nL113: 【41†previous torch.Tensor.scatter_】 【42†next torch.Tensor.scatter_add】\nL114: \nL115: © Copyright PyTorch Contributors.\nL116: \nL117: Built with the 【43†PyData Sphinx Theme†pydata-sphinx-theme.readthedocs.io】 0.15.4.\nL118: \nL119: On this page\nL120: \nL121: 【44†Show Source】\nL122: \nL123: PyTorch Libraries\nL124: \nL125:   * 【45†torchao】\nL126:   * 【46†torchrec】\nL127:   * 【47†torchft】\nL128:   * 【48†TorchCodec】\nL129:   * 【49†torchvision】\nL130:   * 【50†ExecuTorch】\nL131:   * 【51†PyTorch on XLA Devices】\nL132: ## Docs\nL133: \nL134: Access comprehensive developer documentation for PyTorch\nL135: \nL136: 【20†View Docs】\nL137: \nL138: ## Tutorials\nL139: \nL140: Get in-depth tutorials for beginners and advanced developers\nL141: \nL142: 【3†View Tutorials】\nL143: ## Resources\nL144: \nL145: Find development resources and get your questions answered\nL146: \nL147: 【12†View Resources†pytorch.org】\nL148: \nL149: Stay in touch for updates, event info, and the latest news\nL150: \nL151: By submitting this form, I consent to receive marketing emails from the LF and its projects regarding their events, training, research, developments, and related announcements. I understand that I can unsubscribe at any time using the links in the footers of the emails I receive. 【52†Privacy Policy†www.linuxfoundation.org】.\nL152: © PyTorch. Copyright © The Linux Foundation®. All rights reserved. The Linux Foundation has registered trademarks and uses trademarks. For more information, including terms of use, privacy policy, and trademark usage, please see our 【53†Policies†www.linuxfoundation.org】 page. 【54†Trademark Usage†www.linuxfoundation.org】. 【55†Privacy Policy†www.linuxfoundation.org】.\nL153: \nL154: © Copyright PyTorch Contributors.\nL155: \nL156: Created using 【56†Sphinx†www.sphinx-doc.org】 5.3.0.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBroadcasting semantics — PyTorch 2.15 documentation (https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/2.15/notes/broadcasting.html)\nciteturn90view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/2.15/notes/broadcasting.html\",\"lineno\":419}); Total lines: 553\nL373:   * 【5†Developer Notes】\nL374:     * 【87†Automatic Mixed Precision examples】\nL375:     * 【88†Autograd mechanics】\nL376:     * 【89†Broadcasting semantics】\nL377:     * 【90†CPU threading and TorchScript inference】\nL378:     * 【91†CUDA semantics】\nL379:     * 【92†PyTorch Custom Operators Landing Page】\nL380:     * 【93†Distributed Data Parallel】\nL381:     * 【94†Extending PyTorch】\nL382:     * 【95†Extending torch.func with autograd.Function】\nL383:     * 【96†Frequently Asked Questions】\nL384:     * 【97†Getting Started on Intel GPU】\nL385:     * 【98†Gradcheck mechanics】\nL386:     * 【99†HIP (ROCm) semantics】\nL387:     * 【100†Features for large-scale deployments】\nL388:     * 【101†LibTorch Stable ABI】\nL389:     * 【102†LocalTensor Tutorial: Single-Process SPMD Debugging】\nL390:     * 【103†MKLDNN backend】\nL391:     * 【104†Modules】\nL392:     * 【105†MPS backend】\nL393:     * 【106†Multiprocessing best practices】\nL394:     * 【107†Numerical accuracy】\nL395:     * 【108†Out Notes】\nL396:     * 【109†Reproducibility】\nL397:     * 【110†Serialization semantics】\nL398:     * 【111†TensorIterator (Python)】\nL399:     * 【112†Windows FAQ】\nL400: Accelerator Integration\nL401: \nL402:   * 【6†Accelerator Integration】\nL403:     * 【226†Device Management】\nL404:     * 【227†Accelerator Hooks】\nL405:     * 【228†Guard】\nL406:     * 【229†Autoload Mechanism】\nL407:     * 【230†Operator Registration】\nL408:     * 【231†Automatic Mixed Precision】\nL409:     * 【232†Profiler Integration】\nL410:     * 【233†Distributed Training Integration】\nL411:     * 【234†CI Integration】\nL412: # Broadcasting semantics【235†#】\nL413: \nL414: Created On: May 08, 2026 | Last Updated On: May 08, 2026\nL415: \nL416: Many PyTorch operations support NumPy’s broadcasting semantics. See 【236†Numpy Documentation†numpy.org】 for details.\nL417: \nL418: In short, if a PyTorch operation supports broadcast, then its Tensor arguments can be automatically expanded to be of equal sizes (without making copies of the data).\nL419: ## General semantics【237†#】\nL420: \nL421: Two tensors are “broadcastable” if the following rules hold:\nL422: \nL423:   * When iterating over the dimension sizes, starting at the trailing dimension, the dimension sizes must either be equal, one of them is 1, or one of them does not exist.\nL424: For Example:\nL425: \nL426:     >>> x=torch.empty(5,7,3)\nL427:     >>> y=torch.empty(5,7,3)\nL428:     # same shapes are always broadcastable (i.e. the above rules always hold)\nL429: \nL430:     >>> x=torch.empty((0,))\nL431:     >>> y=torch.empty(2,2)\nL432:     # x and y are not broadcastable, because the 0-sized dimension of x\nL433:     # does not match the 2-sized dimension of y.\nL434: \nL435:     # can line up trailing dimensions\nL436:     >>> x=torch.empty(5,3,4,1)\nL437:     >>> y=torch.empty(  3,1,1)\nL438:     # x and y are broadcastable.\nL439:     # 1st trailing dimension: both have size 1\nL440:     # 2nd trailing dimension: y has size 1\nL441:     # 3rd trailing dimension: x size == y size\nL442:     # 4th trailing dimension: y dimension doesn't exist\nL443: \nL444:     # but:\nL445:     >>> x=torch.empty(5,2,4,1)\nL446:     >>> y=torch.empty(  3,1,1)\nL447:     # x and y are not broadcastable, because in the 3rd trailing dimension 2 != 3\nL448: If two tensors `x`, `y` are “broadcastable”, the resulting tensor size is calculated as follows:\nL449: \nL450:   * If the number of dimensions of `x` and `y` are not equal, prepend 1 to the dimensions of the tensor with fewer dimensions to make them equal length.\nL451: \nL452:   * Then, for each dimension size, the resulting dimension size is the max of the sizes of `x` and `y` along that dimension.\nL453: For Example:\nL454: \nL455:     # can line up trailing dimensions to make reading easier\nL456:     >>> x=torch.empty(5,1,4,1)\nL457:     >>> y=torch.empty(  3,1,1)\nL458:     >>> (x+y).size()\nL459:     torch.Size([5, 3, 4, 1])\nL460: \nL461:     # but not necessary:\nL462:     >>> x=torch.empty(1)\nL463:     >>> y=torch.empty(3,1,7)\nL464:     >>> (x+y).size()\nL465:     torch.Size([3, 1, 7])\nL466: \nL467:     >>> x=torch.empty(5,2,4,1)\nL468:     >>> y=torch.empty(3,1,1)\nL469:     >>> (x+y).size()\nL470:     RuntimeError: The size of tensor a (2) must match the size of tensor b (3) at non-singleton dimension 1\nL471: ## In-place semantics【238†#】\nL472: \nL473: One complication is that in-place operations do not allow the in-place tensor to change shape as a result of the broadcast.\nL474: For Example:\nL475: \nL476:     >>> x=torch.empty(5,3,4,1)\nL477:     >>> y=torch.empty(3,1,1)\nL478:     >>> (x.add_(y)).size()\nL479:     torch.Size([5, 3, 4, 1])\nL480: \nL481:     # but:\nL482:     >>> x=torch.empty(1,3,1)\nL483:     >>> y=torch.empty(3,1,7)\nL484:     >>> (x.add_(y)).size()\nL485:     RuntimeError: The expanded size of the tensor (1) must match the existing size (7) at non-singleton dimension 2.\nL486: ## Backwards compatibility【239†#】\nL487: Prior versions of PyTorch allowed certain pointwise functions to execute on tensors with different shapes, as long as the number of elements in each tensor was equal. The pointwise operation would then be carried out by viewing each tensor as 1-dimensional. PyTorch now supports broadcasting and the “1-dimensional” pointwise behavior is considered deprecated and will generate a Python warning in cases where tensors are not broadcastable, but have the same number of elements.\nL488: Note that the introduction of broadcasting can cause backwards incompatible changes in the case where two tensors do not have the same shape, but are broadcastable and have the same number of elements. For Example:\nL489: \nL490:     >>> torch.add(torch.ones(4,1), torch.randn(4))\nL491: would previously produce a Tensor with size: torch.Size([4,1]), but now produces a Tensor with size: torch.Size([4,4]). In order to help identify cases in your code where backwards incompatibilities introduced by broadcasting may exist, you may set `torch.utils.backcompat.broadcast_warning.enabled` to `True`, which will generate a python warning in such cases.\n"
